{
  "analysis": "I found the bug. In `pool_sweep` (line 650), the loop advances by `p += Whsize_hd(hd)` after already skipping additional free blocks with `p += wh * Wosize_hd(hd)` on line 644. For free blocks, `Whsize_hd(hd)` is `1 + wosize`, not the pool block size `wh`. This means the iterator advances by the wrong amount (e.g. by 1 word for a single free block instead of `wh` words), causing it to read garbage from the middle of blocks. Other pool traversal functions like `calc_pool_stats`, `pool_finalise`, and `caml_redarken_pool` all correctly use `p += wh`. The fix is to change line 650 from `p += Whsize_hd(hd)` to `p += wh`.",
  "plan": "Fix line 650 in runtime/shared_heap.c by replacing `p += Whsize_hd(hd)` with `p += wh`. Then build the compiler to verify the fix works.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "sed -n '648,652p' runtime/shared_heap.c\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "sed -i '650s/p += Whsize_hd(hd);/p += wh;/' runtime/shared_heap.c\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "nl -ba runtime/shared_heap.c | sed -n '644,654p'\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "make -j4\n",
      "duration": 30.0
    }
  ]
}
